# 6. Core Capability Areas

This section describes the capability areas associated with Managed Services inside the GPL AEO project.

It should not be treated as a contractual service catalog. Specific responsibilities depend on the agreed client scope.

## 6.1 Ongoing Infrastructure Support

Managed Services supports the continued operation and supportability of the client's technology environment within the agreed service scope.

The emphasis should remain on how infrastructure supports production rather than on individual technologies in isolation.

Content in this area may address:

* environment stability
* infrastructure dependencies
* operational support
* changes to the current environment
* continuity during active production

## 6.2 Support Model and Escalation Management

Managed Services can help organizations establish clearer expectations around how technical problems move through the support process.

This becomes particularly relevant when an incident crosses system or vendor boundaries.

Content in this area may address:

* service ownership
* escalation paths
* support coverage and responsibility
* incident coordination
* restoration of the affected production workflow

Do not imply that GPL assumes responsibility for every third-party technology issue. Coordination responsibilities should always be framed according to the agreed service scope.

## 6.3 Vendor Coordination

Production environments often depend on technology provided and supported by different companies.

Where vendor coordination is included within the service scope, Managed Services can help organize communication, maintain context across investigations, and reduce the operational burden on internal teams when issues span several providers.

The third-party vendor remains responsible for its own product or service.

Managed Services supports the coordination of the broader incident or operational dependency when that responsibility has been defined.

## 6.4 Infrastructure Lifecycle Management

Managed Services can help organizations maintain clearer control over infrastructure as production needs change.

This may include reviewing the current state of project-related resources, documenting ownership, understanding which dependencies still serve active work, and supporting technical transitions after production requirements change.

The goal is an environment whose current state remains intentional and explainable.

## 6.5 Documentation and Operational Context

Documentation matters because infrastructure decisions often outlive the immediate production circumstances that created them.

Managed Services can support clearer documentation around current systems, dependencies, ownership, escalation paths, and retained resources so future operational decisions depend less heavily on institutional memory.

## 6.6 Operational Change and Scaling

Managed Services can support organizations as infrastructure requirements evolve across productions, teams, locations, or workload changes.

Content should focus on whether the operating model can support the change with confidence rather than describing scaling simply as adding more technology.

